          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": "5. A cube of side 4 cm is cut into cubes of side 1 cm. What is the ratio of the surface areas of the original cube and all the cut-out cubes? (Note that there is no change in volume but a big change in the surface area. This property has major consequences in the biological world.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.211,
            0.882,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "6. The surface areas of the three faces of a cuboid that meet at one of the corners of the cuboid are 6 cm<sup>2</sup>, 15 cm<sup>2</sup>, and 10 cm<sup>2</sup> respectively. What is the volume of the cuboid?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.274,
            0.881,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": "\\*7. A cube of side 5 cm is painted on all its faces. If it is sliced into 1 $\\mathbf { c m } ^ { 3 }$ cubes, how many of these 1 cm<sup>3</sup> cubes have"
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.322,
            0.711,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_chart_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.557,
            0.544,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": "(i) exactly three faces painted?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.577,
            0.533,
            0.596
          ],
          "content": "(ii) exactly two faces painted?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.596,
            0.517,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "(iii) exactly one face painted?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.616,
            0.424,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": "(iv) no face painted?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.648,
            0.879,
            0.687
          ],
          "content": "\\*8. Find a cuboid with edges whose lengths are integers (in cm), given that it has a total surface area of exactly 100 cm<sup>2</sup>."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.687,
            0.636,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "(i) Is there more than one such cuboid?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.706,
            0.488,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Can you find them all?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.725,
            0.625,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Show that you have found them all."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.762,
            0.4,
            0.786
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.798,
            0.854,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": "Suppose a swimming pool is being built in your school. Two choices are available for the dimensions of the pool: (A) 8 ft (depth) × 50 ft × 20 ft and (B) 6 ft (depth) × 100 ft × 15 ft. Which option would be suitable/appropriate for your school? Why? What parameters/aspects did you consider to make the choice? [1 cubic foot ≈ 28.3 litres]"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
